PART 1 — FIX BLACK SCREEN AND OPENGL RENDERING

1.1 Critical Rendering Investigation

The application sometimes displays a completely black gameplay screen.

Investigate the underlying rendering pipeline.

Inspect:

- EGL initialization.
- OpenGL ES context creation.
- Surface lifecycle.
- Framebuffer allocation.
- Render target configuration.
- Color buffers.
- Depth buffers.
- Viewport dimensions.
- Shader compilation.
- Shader linking.
- Texture loading.
- Mesh buffer initialization.
- Camera matrices.
- Projection matrices.
- Model matrices.
- Render pass ordering.
- GPU resource management.

Do not simply add a temporary black-screen workaround.

Find and fix the actual cause.

1.2 EGL Lifecycle Management

Handle:

- Application launch.
- Activity pause.
- Activity resume.
- Surface creation.
- Surface destruction.
- EGL context loss.
- EGL context recreation.
- Scene switching.
- Map loading.
- Preview initialization.

When an EGL context is recreated, restore all required GPU resources.

Do not assume that previous GPU resources remain valid.

1.3 Shader Reliability

Implement proper shader validation.

If a shader fails:

- Record the compilation error.
- Identify the affected shader.
- Provide diagnostic information.
- Use a safe fallback shader where possible.

One broken material shader must not make the entire scene black.

1.4 Rendering Diagnostics

Add a development diagnostic system displaying:

- Renderer status.
- EGL status.
- FPS.
- Frame time.
- Shader errors.
- Texture loading errors.
- Model loading errors.
- Framebuffer status.
- GPU resource status.

Do not silently ignore rendering failures.

1.5 Black Screen Acceptance Criteria

Test:

